research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-480089-items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md:5.1 So $c$ is a lower bound of $\{K_k : k \ge N\}$, whence $i_N \ge c$ and $\liminf_k K_k = \sup\{i_n\} \ge i_N \ge c > 0$. [step 4.1, L1]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-480090-items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md:6.1 Kummer's convergence criterion therefore applies and $\sum a_k$ converges, which is claim 1. [step 5.1, step 1.1, L2]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-480091-items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md:7.1 The hypotheses in claims 1 and 2 are precisely those of the two halves of the ratio test for this sequence, and the conclusions agree, so the ratio test for positive terms is the case $\zeta_k = 1$ of Kummer's test. [step 6.1, step 3.2, L5] ∎
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480092:items/thm-ratio-test.md:1.1 Assume $\limsup_{k} q_k < 1$. [assume-case lt]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480093:items/thm-ratio-test.md:1.2 Assume instead $\liminf_{k} q_k > 1$. [assume-case gt]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480094:items/thm-ratio-test.md:1.3 Each $q_k$ is a nonnegative real, being a quotient of a nonnegative real by a positive one, so every $s_n \ge q_n \ge 0$ and hence $\limsup_k q_k \ge 0$. [given, L1, L3]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480095:items/thm-ratio-test.md:2.1 In the case $\limsup_k q_k < 1$ the value $\Lambda := \limsup_k q_k$ therefore lies strictly between the reals $0$ and $1$ inclusive of $0$, so it is a real number; put $t := (\Lambda + 1)/2$, a real with $0 \le \Lambda < t < 1$. [step 1.1, step 1.3, L1, choose]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480096:items/thm-ratio-test.md:2.2 In the case $\liminf_k q_k > 1$, the real $1$ is not an upper bound of $\{i_n\}$, so there is $N \in \mathbb{N}$ with $i_N > 1$. [step 1.2, L1, choose]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480097:items/thm-ratio-test.md:3.1 In the case $\Lambda < 1$: since $t > \Lambda = \inf\{s_n\}$, the real $t$ is not a lower bound of $\{s_n\}$, so there is $N$ with $s_N < t$, and then $q_k \le s_N < t$ for every $k \ge N$. [step 2.1, L1, choose]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480098:items/thm-ratio-test.md:3.2 In the case $\liminf_k q_k > 1$: $q_k \ge i_N > 1$ for every $k \ge N$, since $i_N$ is a lower bound of $\{q_k : k \ge N\}$. [step 2.2, L1]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480099:items/thm-ratio-test.md:3.3 In the case $\Lambda < 1$: the series $\sum_{j \ge 0} t^{\,j}$ converges since $|t| = t < 1$, hence so does $\sum_{j \ge 0} |a_N| t^{\,j}$. [step 2.1, L5]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480100:items/thm-ratio-test.md:4.1 In the case $\Lambda < 1$: for $k \ge N$, $|a_{k+1}|/|a_k| < t$ with $|a_k| > 0$, hence $|a_{k+1}| < t\,|a_k|$. [step 3.1, L3, algebra]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480101:items/thm-ratio-test.md:4.2 In the case $\liminf_k q_k > 1$: for $k \ge N$, $|a_{k+1}| > |a_k|$, again multiplying by $|a_k| > 0$. [step 3.2, L3, algebra]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480102:items/thm-ratio-test.md:5.1 In the case $\Lambda < 1$: an induction on $j$ gives $|a_{N+j}| \le |a_N|\,t^{\,j}$ for every $j \in \mathbb{N}$. At $j = 0$ this is an equality, since $t^{0} = 1$; and if it holds at $j$ then $|a_{N+j+1}| < t\,|a_{N+j}| \le t\,|a_N| t^{\,j} = |a_N| t^{\,j+1}$, using $t > 0$. [step 4.1, L4, L8]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480103:items/thm-ratio-test.md:5.2 In the case $\liminf_k q_k > 1$: an induction on $j$ gives $|a_{N+j}| \ge |a_N| > 0$ for every $j \in \mathbb{N}$. At $j = 0$ it is an equality, and if it holds at $j$ then $|a_{N+j+1}| > |a_{N+j}| \ge |a_N|$. [step 4.2, L3, L4]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480104:items/thm-ratio-test.md:6.1 In the case $\Lambda < 1$: with $x_j := |a_{N+j}|$ and $y_j := |a_N| t^{\,j}$ we have $0 \le x_j \le y_j$ for every $j$, so $\sum_j |a_{N+j}|$ converges; that is the $N$-th tail series of $\sum |a_k|$, so $\sum |a_k|$ converges. [step 5.1, step 3.3, L3, L5, L6]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480105:items/thm-ratio-test.md:6.2 In the case $\liminf_k q_k > 1$: $(a_k)$ does not converge to $0$. Choose a natural $n \ge 1$ with $1/n < |a_N|$; if $a_k \to 0$ there would be $K$ with $|a_k| < 1/n$ for all $k \ge K$, contradicting $|a_k| \ge |a_N| > 1/n$ at any index $k$ that is at least both $K$ and $N$. [step 5.2, L7, choose]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480106:items/thm-ratio-test.md:7.1 In the case $\liminf_k q_k > 1$: by the term test $\sum a_k$ diverges, which is claim 2. [step 6.2, L7]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480107:items/thm-ratio-test.md:7.2 In the case $\Lambda < 1$: the series $\sum |a_k|$ having been shown to converge, $\sum a_k$ converges as well; together with the convergence of $\sum |a_k|$ that is claim 1. [step 6.1, L9]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:480108:items/thm-ratio-test.md:8.1 The two assumed hypotheses are the cases of the disjunction in the Given, and they exhaust it; outside them both claims are vacuous, each hypothesis being false, so the theorem holds for every sequence with nonvanishing terms. [step 7.2, step 7.1, cases-exhaustive] ∎
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-480109-items/thm-kummer-test.md:1.1 Suppose $\liminf_k K_k > 0$. The real $0$ is then not an upper bound of $\{i_n\}$, so there is $N \in \mathbb{N}$ with $i_N > 0$. [given, L1, L2, choose]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-480110-items/thm-kummer-test.md:1.2 Suppose now that $\sum 1/\zeta_k$ diverges and that there is $N$ with $K_k \le 0$ for every $k \ge N$. [given]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-480111-items/thm-kummer-test.md:2.1 Since $i_N \le K_N$ and $i_N > 0$, the value $i_N$ is a real number; put $c := i_N > 0$, so that $K_k \ge c$ for every $k \ge N$. [step 1.1, L1]
