research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-743324-+{"id":"thm-kummer-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"Thomson–Bruckner–Bruckner Theorem 3.37 gives exactly the positive-weight Kummer expression, the positive-liminf convergence criterion, and the eventual-nonpositive plus divergent reciprocal-weight criterion. The proof follows the sourced telescoping comparison, expanded with extended-real tail-infimum details.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-743325-+{"id":"cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"Both textbooks explicitly identify d'Alembert's ratio test as Kummer's test with constant weights. The item states only the two limsup/liminf ratio conclusions recovered in the positive-term setting and proves them through reciprocal bounds, so it is an expanded semantic specialization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
