items/ex-two-continuous-real-functions-agreeing-on-the-rationals-are-equal.md-88-
items/ex-two-continuous-real-functions-agreeing-on-the-rationals-are-equal.md-89-- **Both hypotheses on the codomain and on the maps are needed.** Dropping continuity of one map leaves the conclusion false for the obvious reason, and dropping the Hausdorff condition on the codomain leaves it false for a less obvious one: [[fs-two-continuous-maps-agreeing-on-a-dense-subset-are-equal]] exhibits two continuous maps on $\mathbb{R}$ agreeing at every rational, differing at every irrational, and taking values in a two-point space that is not Hausdorff.
items/ex-two-continuous-real-functions-agreeing-on-the-rationals-are-equal.md-90-
items/ex-two-continuous-real-functions-agreeing-on-the-rationals-are-equal.md:91:- **The rationals enter at one step only.** They are used in step 1.1 and nowhere else; the argument as written proves the statement for an arbitrary dense subset of $\mathbb{R}$ once density in the sense of [[def-dense-top]] is in hand, and step 1.1 is simply where that is established for $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$.
--
