items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-58-## Facts & Assumptions
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-59-
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md:60:**Given:** A sequence $(a_k)$ of reals with $a_k > 0$ for every $k$; the ratios $q_k = a_{k+1}/a_k$, which are positive; the constant weights $\zeta_k = 1$; and Kummer's expression $K_k = a_k/a_{k+1} - 1$ ([[thm-kummer-test]], [[def-limsup-liminf]]).
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-61-
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md:62:[L1] Every subset of $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$ has a least upper bound and a greatest lower bound there ([[lem-extended-reals-complete]], [[def-extended-reals]]); $\limsup_k x_k = \inf\{s_n\}$ and $\liminf_k x_k = \sup\{i_n\}$ with $s_n, i_n$ the tail bounds, and both exist for every sequence ([[def-limsup-liminf]], [[lem-limsup-exists]]).
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-63-
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-64-[L2] Kummer's test, in both halves, for positive terms and positive weights ([[thm-kummer-test]]).
