items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-76-1.1 The constant weights $\zeta_k = 1$ are positive, and the terms $a_k$ are positive, so Kummer's test applies with these data and its expression is $K_k = a_k/a_{k+1} - 1$. [given, L2]
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-77-
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md:78:1.2 Each $q_k$ is positive, so $\limsup_k q_k \ge 0$, every tail supremum being at least $q_n > 0$. [given, L1]
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-79-
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-80-1.3 Suppose instead $\liminf_k q_k > 1$. The real $1$ is not an upper bound of $\{i'_n\}$, the tail infima of $(q_k)$, so there is $N$ with $i'_N > 1$, and then $q_k \ge i'_N > 1$ for every $k \ge N$. [given, L1, choose]
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-81-
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-82-1.4 The weight series $\sum 1/\zeta_k$ is $\sum 1$, whose terms are constantly $1$ and so do not tend to $0$; it diverges. [given, L4]
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-83-
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md:84:2.1 Suppose $\limsup_k q_k < 1$. Then $\Lambda := \limsup_k q_k$ lies between the reals $0$ and $1$ and is therefore real; put $t := (\Lambda+1)/2$, so that $\Lambda < t < 1$ and $t \ge 1/2 > 0$. [step 1.2, L1, choose]
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-85-
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-86-2.2 For $k \ge N$: $a_{k+1}/a_k > 1$ gives $a_{k+1} > a_k > 0$, hence $a_k/a_{k+1} < 1$, hence $K_k < 0$ and in particular $K_k \le 0$. [step 1.3, L3, algebra]
items/cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights.md-87-
