items/ex-ratio-fails-root-succeeds.md-76-
items/ex-ratio-fails-root-succeeds.md:77:1.1 The three values in the display are exactly what [L1] records for this sequence, together with $\liminf_k r_k = \limsup_k r_k = 1/2$, which follows from the convergence of $(r_k)$ to $1/2$. [given, L1, L3]
items/ex-ratio-fails-root-succeeds.md-78-
items/ex-ratio-fails-root-succeeds.md-79-1.2 The sequence is null: $0 < a_k = 2^{-k} t_k \le 2 \cdot (1/2)^{k}$ for every $k$, and $(1/2)^{k} \to 0$ because $|1/2| < 1$, so $2 \cdot (1/2)^{k} \to 0$ and the squeeze gives $a_k \to 0$. [given, L4, L5, L6]
items/ex-ratio-fails-root-succeeds.md-80-
items/ex-ratio-fails-root-succeeds.md:81:2.1 The ratio quantities differ from one another and from the root quantities: $1/8 < 1/2 < 2$, so $\liminf_k q_k < \liminf_k r_k = \limsup_k r_k < \limsup_k q_k$. In particular the chain [L2] holds here with both outer inequalities strict and the middle one an equality, and the ratios do not determine the roots. [step 1.1, L1, L2, L6]
items/ex-ratio-fails-root-succeeds.md-82-
items/ex-ratio-fails-root-succeeds.md:83:3.1 So $(a_k)$ is a positive null sequence whose root sequence converges to $1/2 < 1$ while its ratio sequence has $\limsup_k q_k = 2 > 1$ and $\liminf_k q_k = 1/8 < 1$, that is, the ratios oscillate across $1$ while the roots settle strictly below it. [step 1.2, step 2.1, L1, L6] ∎
