items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-62-[L4] In a complete ordered field every $a \ge 0$ has a unique $s \ge 0$ with $s^2 = a$ ([[thm-of-square-roots]]).
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-63-
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md:64:[L5] No rational squares to $2$ ([[fs-sqrt2-rational]]); the map $q \mapsto \hat q$ is an injective embedding of ordered fields, so it preserves sums, products and the order ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]], [[def-rationals]]).
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-65-
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-66-[L6] Squaring is strictly monotone on the nonnegatives: $0 \le a < b$ gives $a^2 < b^2$ ([[lem-of-square-monotone]]); $0 < 1$ and the order is total and transitive ([[cor-of-one-positive]], [[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-67-
--
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-73-
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-74-1.1 By [L4] there is a unique real $r \ge 0$ with $r^2 = 2$, and $0 < r < 2$: indeed $r \ne 0$ since $0^2 = 0 \ne 2$, while $r \ge 2$ would give $r^2 \ge 4 > 2$ by [L6]. [L4, L6]
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-75-
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md:76:1.2 $r \notin \mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$: if $r = \hat q$ for a rational $q$, then $\widehat{q^2} = \hat q \cdot \hat q = r^2 = 2 = \hat 2$ by [L5], and injectivity of the embedding gives $q^2 = 2$ in $\mathbb{Q}$, contradicting [L5]. [L5]
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-77-
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-78-1.3 $E$ is bounded, since $0 \le y \le 2$ for every $y \in E$ by the definition of $E$. [given, L7]
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-79-
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md:80:2.1 Put $A := E \cap (-\infty, r)$ and $B := E \cap (r, \infty)$. Then $A \cup B = E$, because every $y \in E$ satisfies $y \ne r$ by step 1.2 and hence $y < r$ or $y > r$; and both are nonempty, since $0 \in A$ and $2 \in B$ by step 1.1, both being rationals in $[0,2]$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L3, L5, L6]
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-81-
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-82-3.1 $A$ and $B$ are separated: $(-\infty, r]$ is closed and contains $A$, so $\overline{A} \subseteq (-\infty,r]$ by [L2] and hence $\overline{A} \cap B \subseteq (-\infty,r] \cap (r,\infty) = \varnothing$; symmetrically $\overline{B} \subseteq [r,\infty)$ and $A \cap \overline{B} = \varnothing$. Hence $(A,B)$ is a disconnection of $E$ and $E$ is disconnected, so the claim [A1] is refuted. [step 2.1, A1, L1, L2, L3] ∎
items/cex-rationals-in-an-interval-are-disconnected.md-83-
--
items/lem-rational-cut-is-cut.md-38-
items/lem-rational-cut-is-cut.md-39-## Facts & Assumptions
items/lem-rational-cut-is-cut.md-40-
items/lem-rational-cut-is-cut.md:41:**Given:** A rational $q$ and the set $q^{*} = \{\, r \in \mathbb{Q} : r < q \,\}$, with the Dedekind-cut axioms (C1) proper and nonempty, (C2) downward closed, (C3) no greatest element ([[def-dedekind-cut]]).
items/lem-rational-cut-is-cut.md-42-
items/lem-rational-cut-is-cut.md-43-[L1] $\mathbb{Q}$ is a totally ordered field: $<$ is transitive and total, $q - 1 < q < q + 1$, and whenever $p < q$ the midpoint $\tfrac{p+q}{2}$ satisfies $p < \tfrac{p+q}{2} < q$ ([[thm-rat-ordered-field]]).
