items/cex-subset-closed-under-the-operation-not-a-subgroup.md-62-
items/cex-subset-closed-under-the-operation-not-a-subgroup.md-63-1.1 $0 \in H$, since $0 \le 0$; so $H$ is nonempty and contains the identity of $(\mathbb{Z},+)$. [L1, given]
items/cex-subset-closed-under-the-operation-not-a-subgroup.md-64-
items/cex-subset-closed-under-the-operation-not-a-subgroup.md:65:1.2 $H$ is closed under addition: if $0 \le x$ and $0 \le y$ then $x \le x + y$ by compatibility of the order with addition, and $0 \le x$, so $0 \le x + y$ by transitivity. Hence $+$ restricts to a binary operation on $H$, and $(H,+,0)$ is a commutative monoid. [L1, L4, given]
items/cex-subset-closed-under-the-operation-not-a-subgroup.md-66-
items/cex-subset-closed-under-the-operation-not-a-subgroup.md-67-1.3 $0 < 1$: the integer $1 = \iota(1)$ lies in the image of $\iota$, so $0 \le 1$, and $1 \ne 0$ because $\iota$ is injective and $1 \ne 0$ in $\mathbb{N}$. Hence $1 \in H$. [L2, given]
items/cex-subset-closed-under-the-operation-not-a-subgroup.md-68-
