items/cex-subset-closed-under-the-operation-not-a-subgroup.md-71-3.1 Therefore $H$ is not closed under inverses, since $1 \in H$ and its additive inverse $-1$ is not in $H$; so $H$ is not a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z},+)$. [step 1.3, step 2.1, L3]
items/cex-subset-closed-under-the-operation-not-a-subgroup.md-72-
items/cex-subset-closed-under-the-operation-not-a-subgroup.md:73:4.1 By steps 1.1, 1.2 and 3.1 the set $H$ is a nonempty subset containing the identity and closed under the operation which is not a subgroup, so the claim of [L5] is false. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 3.1, L5] ∎
