items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-47-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-48-1. **In the exponent.** If $a > 1$ then $a^{r} < a^{s}$; if $a = 1$ then $a^{r} = a^{s} = 1$; if $0 < a < 1$ then $a^{r} > a^{s}$.
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-49-2. **In the base.** If $r \in \mathbb{Q}$ with $r > 0$ and $0 < a < b$, then $a^{r} < b^{r}$; so $a \mapsto a^{r}$ is strictly increasing on $\{a \in \mathbb{R} : a > 0\}$.
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md:50:3. **Comparison with $1$.** For rational $t > 0$: $a > 1$ implies $a^{t} > 1$, and $0 < a < 1$ implies $a^{t} < 1$.
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-51-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-52-## Facts & Assumptions
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-53-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-54-**Given:** A real $a > 0$ and rationals $r < s$; write $t := s - r$, a rational with $t > 0$.
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-55-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md:56:[L1] Positive rationals have positive representatives: $t > 0$ can be written $t = m/N$ with $m, N$ naturals and $m \ge 1$, $N \ge 1$. Every rational has a representative with positive denominator ([[lem-rat-positive-denominator]]); on such a representative $m/N > 0$ holds exactly when $m > 0$ in $\mathbb{Z}$ ([[def-rat-order]], [[def-int-order]]); and a positive integer is the image of a unique natural $\ge 1$ ([[lem-nat-embeds-int]]), which is what turns both the numerator $m > 0$ and the denominator $N > 0$ into naturals $\ge 1$. The last passage is a genuine step and is what [[lem-rational-power-well-defined]] uses at its own [L5].
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-57-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md:58:[L2] Rational power laws ([[lem-rational-power-laws]], [[lem-rational-power-well-defined]], [[def-rational-power]]): $a^{r} > 0$; $a^{r+t} = a^{r} a^{t}$; and $a^{t} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m}$ for the representative $t = m/N$.
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-59-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-60-[L3] Roots ([[thm-nth-roots-exist]]): $a^{1/N}$ is the unique $x \ge 0$ with $x^{N} = a$, and it is $> 0$ when $a > 0$.
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-61-
--
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-67-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-68-**Proof technique:** cases.
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-69-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md:70:1.1 Write $t = s - r$, so $t$ is rational with $t > 0$, and fix a representative $t = m/N$ with naturals $m, N \ge 1$; then $a^{s} = a^{r+t} = a^{r} a^{t}$ with $a^{r} > 0$, so the comparison of $a^{s}$ with $a^{r}$ is exactly the comparison of $a^{t}$ with $1$. [L1, L2, L5]
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-71-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md:72:1.2 Claim 2, which needs no case split: let $r > 0$ be rational with representative $r = m/N$, $m, N \ge 1$, and let $0 < a < b$; then $a^{1/N} < b^{1/N}$, since $b^{1/N} \le a^{1/N}$ would give $b = \big(b^{1/N}\big)^{N} \le \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{N} = a$; raising to the power $m$ preserves the strict inequality between nonnegatives, so $a^{r} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m} < \big(b^{1/N}\big)^{m} = b^{r}$. [L2, L3, L4]
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-73-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-74-2.1 Case $a > 1$: then $a^{1/N} > 1$, because $a^{1/N} \le 1$ would give $a = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{N} \le 1$; hence $a^{t} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m} \ge a^{1/N} > 1$, and multiplying $1 < a^{t}$ by $a^{r} > 0$ gives $a^{r} < a^{r} a^{t} = a^{s}$. [assume-case big, step 1.1, L3, L4, L5]
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-75-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md:76:2.2 Case $a = 1$: then $1^{N} = 1$ shows $a^{1/N} = 1$ by uniqueness of the nonnegative $N$-th root, so $a^{u} = 1^{m'} = 1$ for every rational $u$ with representative $m'/N'$; in particular $a^{r} = a^{s} = 1$. [assume-case one, step 1.1, L2, L3, L4]
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-77-
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-78-2.3 Case $0 < a < 1$: then $0 \le a^{1/N} < 1$, because $a^{1/N} \ge 1$ would give $a = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{N} \ge 1$; hence $a^{t} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m} < 1^{m} = 1$ by strict monotonicity of $x \mapsto x^{m}$ on the nonnegatives, $m \ge 1$, and multiplying $a^{t} < 1$ by $a^{r} > 0$ gives $a^{s} = a^{r} a^{t} < a^{r}$. [assume-case small, step 1.1, L3, L4, L5]
items/lem-rational-power-monotone.md-79-
--
