items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-83-
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-84-1.3 For all reals $a < b$ the interval $(a,b)$ is uncountable. [L7]
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-85-
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md:86:2.1 For all reals $a < b$ the interval $(a,b)$ contains an irrational: if it did not, then $(a,b) \subseteq \mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$, so $(a,b)$ would be a subset of an at most countable set by step 1.2 and hence at most countable by [L6], contradicting step 1.3. So some $z \in (a,b)$ lies in $X$. [step 1.2, step 1.3, L6]
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-87-
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-88-2.2 Every nonempty open $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is uncountable: fix $x \in U$ and, by [L3], a real $\varepsilon > 0$ with $N_\varepsilon(x) \subseteq U$; by [L2] the set $N_\varepsilon(x)$ is the interval $(x - \varepsilon, x + \varepsilon)$ with $x - \varepsilon < x + \varepsilon$, hence uncountable by step 1.3. Were $U$ at most countable, its subset $N_\varepsilon(x)$ would be at most countable by [L6], which it is not; so $U$ is uncountable, which is claim 3. [step 1.3, L2, L3, L6, choose]
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-89-
--
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-107-  A nonempty set with empty interior can perfectly well be countable, as
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-108-  $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$ shows.
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-109-
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md:110:- **An explicit irrational is not produced here.** Step 2.1 is a counting
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-111-  argument and exhibits nothing. The library does exhibit one separately,
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-112-  $\sqrt 2$ ([[thm-of-square-roots]], [[fs-sqrt2-rational]]), and an explicit
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-113-  irrational in a given interval $(a,b)$ can be built from it as
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md:114:  $q_1 + (q_2 - q_1)/\sqrt 2$ for suitable rationals $q_1 < q_2$ in the
items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md-115-  interval; that route is longer and is not the one taken above.
