items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-76-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-77-## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-78-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md:79:**Given:** A natural $n \ge 1$, reals $x_0, \dots, x_{n-1} > 0$, and rationals $w_0, \dots, w_{n-1} \ge 0$ with $\sum_{k<n} \iota(w_k) = 1$, a sum in $\mathbb{R}$.
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-80-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-81-[L1] AM-GM ([[thm-am-gm]]): for $c_0, \dots, c_{N-1} \ge 0$ with $N \ge 1$, $\big(\prod_{i<N} c_i\big)^{1/N} \le \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i<N} c_i$.
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-82-
--
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-86-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-87-[L4] Rational power laws ([[lem-rational-power-laws]], [[def-rational-power]], [[thm-nth-roots-exist]]): for $u, v > 0$ and rationals $r, s$: $u^{r} > 0$, $(uv)^{r} = u^{r}v^{r}$ (hence, by induction on the number of factors, $\big(\prod_{j<n} u_j\big)^{r} = \prod_{j<n} u_j^{\,r}$, using also the constant product for the empty case), and $\big(u^{r}\big)^{s} = u^{rs}$.
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-88-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md:89:[L5] Rational arithmetic, the order on $\mathbb{Q}$ and the embedding ([[lem-rat-positive-denominator]], [[def-rationals]], [[def-rat-operations]], [[def-rat-order]], [[lem-of-q-embeds]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]): on a representative with positive denominator, $m/N \ge 0$ holds exactly when $m \ge 0$ in $\mathbb{Z}$ ([[def-rat-order]], with the order on $\mathbb{Z}$ as in [[def-int-order]]); a nonnegative integer is the image of a unique natural and a positive integer the image of a unique natural $\ge 1$ ([[lem-nat-embeds-int]]), which is the step that licenses reading such an $m$, and such a denominator, as a natural number; every single rational has a representative with positive denominator ([[lem-rat-positive-denominator]] states exactly this, for one rational; the passage to a common denominator for finitely many is NOT quoted from it and is carried out by the induction inside step 1.1); $\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{R}$ is an injective order-preserving field homomorphism, so $\iota(0) = 0$, $\iota(u + v) = \iota(u) + \iota(v)$ and $\iota(uv) = \iota(u)\iota(v)$; on an integer $\iota$ is $m \mapsto m \cdot 1_\mathbb{R}$, with $\iota(m) > 0$ for a natural $m \ge 1$ and $m \mapsto \iota(m)$ injective on $\{1, 2, \dots\}$, hence injective on all of $\mathbb{N}$ since $\iota(0) = 0 < \iota(m)$ there.
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-90-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-91-[L6] Induction principle ([[thm-induction-principle]]), used for the routine inductions on the number of terms below, and the recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]), which is what defines a function on $\mathbb{N}$ by a recursion clause.
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-92-
--
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-94-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-95-**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-96-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md:97:1.1 Common denominator, by an induction written out rather than asserted. The claim at $j$ is: any rationals $v_0, \dots, v_{j-1}$ admit a natural $N \ge 1$ and integers $p_0, \dots, p_{j-1}$ with $v_k = p_k/N$ for every $k < j$. At $j = 0$ take $N = 1$, there being no $p_k$ to produce. Assume the claim at $j$ and let $v_0, \dots, v_j$ be given: applying it to $v_0, \dots, v_{j-1}$ yields a natural $N' \ge 1$ and integers $p'_k$ with $v_k = p'_k/N'$, and $v_j$ has a representative $v_j = a/b$ with $b > 0$, hence with $b$ a natural $\ge 1$; then $N := N'b$ is a natural $\ge 1$, and the integers $p_k := p'_k b$ for $k < j$ and $p_j := a N'$ satisfy $p_k/N = (p'_k b)/(N' b) = p'_k/N' = v_k$ and $p_j/N = (aN')/(bN') = a/b = v_j$, since $(x, y) \sim (xc, yc)$ for $c \ne 0$. The claim at $j = n$, applied to $w_0, \dots, w_{n-1}$, fixes a natural $N \ge 1$ and integers $m_0, \dots, m_{n-1}$ with $w_k = m_k/N$. Finally each $m_k$ is a natural: $w_k \ge 0$ read on the positive-denominator representative $m_k/N$ gives $m_k \ge 0$ in $\mathbb{Z}$, and a nonnegative integer is the image of a unique natural. [L5, L6]
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-98-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-99-2.1 The numerators sum to $\iota(N)$ in $\mathbb{R}$: applying scaling to the real sequence $k \mapsto \iota(w_k)$ multiplies the hypothesis $\sum_{k<n} \iota(w_k) = 1$ by $\iota(N)$ to give $\sum_{k<n} \iota(N)\iota(w_k) = \iota(N)$, and $\iota(N)\iota(w_k) = \iota(N w_k) = \iota(m_k)$ since $\iota$ is multiplicative and $N w_k = m_k$ in $\mathbb{Q}$, so $\sum_{k<n} \iota(m_k) = \iota(N)$. [step 1.1, L2, L5]
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-100-
--
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-108-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-109-6.1 Applying AM-GM to $c_0, \dots, c_{N-1}$ and substituting: $\Big(\prod_{j<n} x_j^{\,m_j}\Big)^{1/N} = \Big(\prod_{i<N} c_i\Big)^{1/N} \le \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i<N} c_i = \iota(N)^{-1}\sum_{j<n} \iota(m_j) x_j = \sum_{j<n} \iota(w_j) x_j$, the last equality by scaling together with $\iota(N)^{-1}\iota(m_j) = \iota(m_j/N) = \iota(w_j)$. [step 4.1, step 5.1, L1, L2, L5]
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-110-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md:111:6.2 Rewriting the left-hand side with the rational power laws: $\Big(\prod_{j<n} x_j^{\,m_j}\Big)^{1/N} = \prod_{j<n} \Big(x_j^{\,m_j}\Big)^{1/N} = \prod_{j<n} x_j^{\,m_j/N} = \prod_{j<n} x_j^{\,w_j}$, each $x_j^{\,m_j}$ being positive. [step 5.1, L4, L6]
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-112-
items/thm-weighted-am-gm-rational.md-113-7.1 Combining the two displays gives $\prod_{j<n} x_j^{\,w_j} \le \sum_{j<n} \iota(w_j) x_j$, which is the assertion. [step 6.1, step 6.2] ∎
--
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-59-
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-60-**Given:** Reals $a, b > 0$ and rationals $r, s$.
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-61-
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md:62:[L1] Definition and well-definedness ([[def-rational-power]], [[lem-rational-power-well-defined]]): for ANY representative $r = m/N$ with $m \in \mathbb{Z}$ and $N \ge 1$ natural, $a^{r} = \big(a^{1/N}\big)^{m}$; and $a^{1/N}$ is the unique $x \ge 0$ with $x^{N} = a$ ([[thm-nth-roots-exist]]), which is $> 0$ when $a > 0$.
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-63-
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-64-[L2] Laws of integer exponents ([[lem-power-laws]], [[def-integer-power]]): for $x, y \ne 0$ and integers $j, k$, $x^{j+k} = x^{j}x^{k}$, $(x^{j})^{k} = x^{jk}$, $(xy)^{j} = x^{j}y^{j}$ and $x^{-j} = (x^{j})^{-1}$.
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-65-
--
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-69-
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-70-[L5] The order on $\mathbb{Q}$ ([[thm-rat-ordered-field]], [[def-rat-order]]) is compatible with addition, so $r > 0$ and $s > 0$ imply $r + s > 0$.
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-71-
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md:72:[L6] The supplementary clause of [[def-rational-power]]: $0^{t} = 0$ for every rational $t > 0$, while $0^{t}$ is left undefined for rational $t < 0$ and the convention $0^{0} = 1$ of [[def-integer-power]] is untouched. In a field, a product with a factor $0$ is $0$ ([[lem-of-zero-mult]]).
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-73-
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-74-## Proof
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-75-
--
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-89-
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-90-3.3 Claim 5: write $r = m/n$ and $s = p/q$ with $n, q \ge 1$, and put $x := a^{1/(nq)}$, so $x > 0$ and $x^{nq} = a$; then $\big(x^{q}\big)^{n} = x^{qn} = a$ with $x^{q} > 0$, so $x^{q} = a^{1/n}$ by uniqueness of the nonnegative $n$-th root; putting $z := x^{m}$ we get $z > 0$ and $z^{q} = \big(x^{m}\big)^{q} = \big(x^{q}\big)^{m} = \big(a^{1/n}\big)^{m} = a^{r}$, so $z$ is the nonnegative $q$-th root of $a^{r}$, that is $z = \big(a^{r}\big)^{1/q}$; therefore $\big(a^{r}\big)^{s} = \Big(\big(a^{r}\big)^{1/q}\Big)^{p} = z^{p} = \big(x^{m}\big)^{p} = x^{mp} = \big(a^{1/(nq)}\big)^{mp} = a^{(mp)/(nq)} = a^{rs}$. [step 2.1, L1, L2, L3, L4]
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-91-
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md:92:3.4 The supplementary nonnegative case, product identity: let $a, b \ge 0$ and let $r > 0$ be rational; if $a > 0$ and $b > 0$ this is step 2.2, and otherwise $a = 0$ or $b = 0$, so $ab = 0$ and the left side is $0^{r} = 0$, while the right side $a^{r}b^{r}$ has a factor $0^{r} = 0$ and is therefore $0$ as well. [step 2.2, L6]
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-93-
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-94-4.1 The supplementary nonnegative case, addition identity: the identity $a^{r+s} = a^{r}a^{s}$ involves the base $a$ only, so nothing need be assumed about $b$; for $a > 0$ it is step 3.1 verbatim, which uses only $a > 0$, and both sides are then positive rather than $0$; for $a = 0$ the exponents satisfy $r + s > 0$, so the left side is $0^{r+s} = 0$ and the right side is $0 \cdot 0 = 0$. [step 3.1, L5, L6]
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md-95-
items/lem-rational-power-laws.md:96:5.1 All five claims hold for positive bases and arbitrary rational exponents, together with the two supplementary identities for nonnegative bases and positive rational exponents. [step 2.1, step 3.1, step 3.2, step 2.2, step 3.3, step 3.4, step 4.1] ∎
--
