items/cex-ratio-inconclusive-root-decides.md-92-
items/cex-ratio-inconclusive-root-decides.md-93-## Remarks
items/cex-ratio-inconclusive-root-decides.md-94-
items/cex-ratio-inconclusive-root-decides.md-95-- **The two families are computed once, on the previous page, and cited here.** The four limit quantities for this sequence are established in the refutation of [[fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree]], where the same witness shows that the outer inequalities of the ratio-to-root chain are strict. Nothing is recomputed here; what is added is the reading of those numbers through the two tests.
items/cex-ratio-inconclusive-root-decides.md-96-
items/cex-ratio-inconclusive-root-decides.md:97:- **Why the roots behave and the ratios do not.** The exponent of $a_k$ is $-k + (-1)^{k}$. Taking an $(k+1)$-st root divides that exponent by $k+1$, so the bounded oscillation contributes $2^{\pm 1/(k+1)}$, which tends to $1$; forming a ratio differences the exponent, and a bounded oscillation does not shrink under differencing but doubles.
items/cex-ratio-inconclusive-root-decides.md-98-
items/cex-ratio-inconclusive-root-decides.md-99-- **This does not make the root test universal.** The companion counterexample with root limit exactly $1$ shows the root test has its own blind spot, and [[fs-universal-comparison-series]] shows no test on this page can avoid having one.
--
items/thm-metric-hausdorff-separation.md-51-
items/thm-metric-hausdorff-separation.md-52-**Given:** A metric space $(X,d)$ and points $p, q \in X$ with $p \ne q$; write $c := d(p,q)$.
items/thm-metric-hausdorff-separation.md-53-
items/thm-metric-hausdorff-separation.md:54:[L1] Separation (M1), symmetry (M2) and the triangle inequality (M3) of a metric ([[def-metric-space]]); and $d \ge 0$ ([[lem-metric-nonnegativity]]).
items/thm-metric-hausdorff-separation.md-55-
items/thm-metric-hausdorff-separation.md-56-[L2] Halving. For a real $c > 0$ put $2 := 1 + 1$ and $c/2 := c \cdot 2^{-1}$. Then $2 > 0$, so $2 \ne 0$ and $2^{-1} > 0$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]], [[lem-of-add-order]], [[lem-of-inverse-positive]], [[def-ordered-field]]); hence $c/2 > 0$ ([[lem-of-sign-rules]]); and $c/2 + c/2 = c(2 \cdot 2^{-1}) = c$ ([[def-field]]).
items/thm-metric-hausdorff-separation.md-57-
