items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-50-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-51-## Facts & Assumptions
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-52-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md:53:**Given:** For $n \in \mathbb{N}$ the rational $s_n = k_n/10^n$, where $k_n$ is the largest natural with $k_n^2 \le 2 \cdot 10^{2n}$, together with the properties established for it in [[fs-rationals-complete]]; and the real $\sqrt 2$ ([[thm-of-square-roots]]). Rationals are identified with their images in $\mathbb{R}$ under the embedding $q \mapsto \hat q$ ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]), so $(s_n)$ is also a sequence of reals ([[def-sequence]]).
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-54-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-55-[L1] The construction and its properties: $s_n \ge 0$, $s_n^2 \le 2 < (s_n + 10^{-n})^2$, and $(s_n)$ is a Cauchy sequence of rationals with no rational limit ([[fs-rationals-complete]]).
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-56-
--
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-68-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-69-[L8] Every convergent sequence of reals is Cauchy ([[lem-convergent-implies-cauchy]]); every Cauchy sequence of reals converges ([[thm-cauchy-criterion-via-lub]]); limits are unique ([[lem-limit-unique]]).
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-70-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md:71:[L9] No rational squares to $2$ ([[fs-sqrt2-rational]]).
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-72-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-73-## Counterexample
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-74-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-75-**Proof technique:** direct.
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-76-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md:77:1.1 The inequalities of [L1] hold verbatim in $\mathbb{R}$, since the embedding preserves the order and the field operations. [given, L1, L2]
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-78-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-79-1.2 $0 < 1/10 < 1$ and $(1/10)^n = 10^{-n}$ for every $n$. [given, L4, L5]
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-80-
--
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-86-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-87-4.1 In particular $(s_n)$ converges in $\mathbb{R}$ and is therefore Cauchy as a sequence of reals; this is the behaviour [[thm-cauchy-criterion-via-lub]] guarantees for every Cauchy sequence of reals, and it is what fails in $\mathbb{Q}$. [step 3.1, L8]
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-88-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md:89:4.2 Suppose $(s_n)$ converged to a rational $q$. Then in $\mathbb{R}$ it converges to $\hat q$, so $\hat q = \sqrt 2$ by uniqueness of limits, hence $\hat q^{\,2} = 2$; the embedding is injective and preserves squaring, so $q^2 = 2$ in $\mathbb{Q}$. [step 3.1, L2, L3, L8]
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-90-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md:91:5.1 No rational squares to $2$, so no such $q$ exists: $(s_n)$ is a Cauchy sequence of rationals with no rational limit, and the claim that $\mathbb{Q}$ is complete is refuted. [step 4.2, L1, L9] ∎
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-92-
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-93-## Remarks
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-94-
--
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-100-- **Decimal truncation is a convenience, not the mechanism.** Any sequence of
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-101-  rationals converging to any irrational does the same job, for instance the
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-102-  Babylonian iterates of [[ex-babylonian-sqrt-two]] started at $2$, which are all
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md:103:  rational and converge to $\sqrt 2$. Truncated decimals are chosen because the
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-104-  two-sided estimate $s_n \le \sqrt 2 < s_n + 10^{-n}$ is immediate from the
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-105-  definition of $k_n$ and turns into convergence with one application of the
items/cex-cauchy-rationals-no-rational-limit.md-106-  squeeze theorem.
--
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md-32-
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md-33-## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md-34-
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md:35:**Given:** A positive real $a$ and a rational $r=p/q$ with $q\ge1$.
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md-36-
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md-37-[L1] Rational powers satisfy $(a^{p/q})^q=a^p$, are positive for positive base, and obey the rational power laws ([[def-rational-power]], [[lem-rational-power-laws]]).
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md-38-
--
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md-50-
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md-51-2.1 Injectivity of $\log$ gives $\log(a^{p/q})=(p/q)\log a$, and exponentiating gives $a^{p/q}=\exp((p/q)\log a)$. [step 1.1, L2, L3]
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md-52-
items/thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent.md:53:3.1 This is the new real power at exponent $p/q$; negative rational exponents follow by reciprocals, and the stated $0^r$ convention agrees with [[def-rational-power]] for $r>0$. [step 2.1, L1, L3] ∎
