items/thm-p-series-rational.md-62-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-63-## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-64-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md:65:**Given:** A rational $p > 0$ and the family $a_k := 1/k^{p} = \iota(k)^{-p}$, defined for naturals $k \ge 1$ ([[def-rational-power]], [[def-series]]).
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-66-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md:67:[L1] Rational powers of a positive base are positive, and $a^{r+s} = a^{r}a^{s}$, $(a^{r})^{s} = a^{rs}$, $a^{-r} = 1/a^{r}$ for $a > 0$ and rationals $r, s$ ([[lem-rational-power-laws]]).
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-68-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md:69:[L2] Monotonicity of rational powers: for rational $t > 0$ and $0 < a < b$ one has $a^{t} < b^{t}$; and for $a > 1$ and rationals $r < s$ one has $a^{r} < a^{s}$ ([[lem-rational-power-monotone]]).
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-70-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md:71:[L3] The integer power and the rational power agree at an integer exponent: for $a > 0$ and $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, $a^{n}$ read as in [[def-integer-power]] equals $a^{n}$ read as in [[def-rational-power]], since $n = n/1$ and $a^{1/1} = a$ ([[thm-nth-roots-exist]], [[def-rational-power]]). In particular $a^{0} = 1$.
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-72-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-73-[L4] Reciprocation reverses the order on the positives: $0 < a < b$ implies $0 < 1/b < 1/a$ ([[lem-of-inverse-positive]]).
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-74-
--
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-86-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-87-1.2 For naturals $1 \le j < k$ we have $0 < \iota(j) < \iota(k)$, hence $\iota(j)^{p} < \iota(k)^{p}$ since $p > 0$, hence $a_j = 1/\iota(j)^{p} > 1/\iota(k)^{p} = a_k$; and for $j = k$ the two are equal. So $a_j \ge a_k$ whenever $1 \le j \le k$. [given, L7, L2, L4, L1]
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-88-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md:89:1.3 For every $j \in \mathbb{N}$ the base $2^{j}$ is positive and, reading the exponent $j$ as a rational, $2^{j} a_{2^{j}} = 2^{j} \big(2^{j}\big)^{-p} = 2^{j} \cdot 2^{-jp} = 2^{\,j - jp} = 2^{\,(1-p)j} = \big(2^{\,1-p}\big)^{j}$. [L1, L3, L7, algebra]
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-90-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-91-1.4 Since $2 > 1$, the map $t \mapsto 2^{t}$ is strictly increasing on $\mathbb{Q}$ and $2^{0} = 1$; hence $r = 2^{\,1-p} < 1 = 2^{0}$ holds exactly when $1 - p < 0$, that is exactly when $p > 1$. [L2, L3, L7]
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-92-
--
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-100-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-101-## Remarks
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-102-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md:103:- **Where the threshold comes from.** Condensation turns the $p$-series into a geometric series of ratio $2^{1-p}$, and the geometric threshold $r = 1$ pulls back to $p = 1$. Nothing about the number $1$ is special to the $p$-series; it is the exponent at which the condensed terms stop shrinking.
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-104-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-105-- **At $p = 1$ the condensed series is $\sum_{j \ge 0} 1$.** Its terms do not tend to $0$, so it diverges, and with it the harmonic series. That instance is worked out on the companion page, together with the older block argument that does not use condensation at all.
items/thm-p-series-rational.md-106-
items/thm-p-series-rational.md:107:- **Only rational exponents are covered, and the gap is real.** For irrational $p$ the expression $k^{p}$ has no meaning in this library yet, so the statement is not merely unproved there, it is unstatable. The same limitation is what keeps the Bertrand-type series $\sum 1/(k (\log k)^{p})$ off this page entirely, the logarithm not being available.
--
items/fs-rationals-complete.md-42-
items/fs-rationals-complete.md-43-[L1] Ordered-field arithmetic in $\mathbb{Q}$ ([[thm-rat-ordered-field]], [[lem-rat-triangle]]).
items/fs-rationals-complete.md-44-
items/fs-rationals-complete.md:45:[L2] No rational squares to $2$ ([[fs-sqrt2-rational]]).
items/fs-rationals-complete.md-46-
items/fs-rationals-complete.md-47-[L3] A constant sequence is null only if the constant is $0$; sums of null sequences are null; a Cauchy multiple of a null sequence is null ([[def-null-sequence]], [[lem-null-ideal]]).
items/fs-rationals-complete.md-48-
--
items/fs-rationals-complete.md-58-
items/fs-rationals-complete.md-59-2.2 $(s_n^2 - 2)$ is null: $0 \le 2 - s_n^2 < (s_n + 10^{-n})^2 - s_n^2 = 10^{-n}(2 s_n + 10^{-n}) \le 5 \cdot 10^{-n}$. [step 1.1, L1, L4]
items/fs-rationals-complete.md-60-
items/fs-rationals-complete.md:61:3.1 If $(s_n)$ converged to a rational $q$, then $(s_n - q)$ would be null; since $(s_n + q)$ is Cauchy, $(s_n^2 - q^2) = (s_n - q)(s_n + q)$ would be null; adding the null $(2 - s_n^2)$, the constant $2 - q^2$ would be null, forcing $q^2 = 2$. [step 2.1, step 2.2, L3, L1]
items/fs-rationals-complete.md-62-
items/fs-rationals-complete.md:63:4.1 No rational squares to $2$, so $(s_n)$ is a Cauchy sequence of rationals with no rational limit: the claim is refuted. [step 3.1, step 2.1, L2] ∎
--
