items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-62-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-63-## Facts & Assumptions
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-64-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:65:**Given:** A sequence $(a_k)$ of reals with $a_k \ne 0$ for every $k$, the ratios $q_k = |a_{k+1}|/|a_k|$ and the roots $\rho_k = |a_{k+1}|^{1/(k+1)}$ ([[def-limsup-liminf]], [[def-extended-reals]]).
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-66-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:67:[L1] For a sequence $(b_k)$ of reals with $b_k > 0$ for every $k$, writing $q'_k = b_{k+1}/b_k$ and $r_k = b_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)}$, one has $\liminf_k q'_k \le \liminf_k r_k \le \limsup_k r_k \le \limsup_k q'_k$ in $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$ ([[thm-ratio-root-inequality]]).
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-68-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:69:[L2] $\liminf_k x_k \le \limsup_k x_k$ for every real sequence ([[lem-liminf-le-limsup]]).
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-70-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-71-[L3] Absolute value: $|x| \ge 0$, and $|x| = 0$ exactly when $x = 0$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-72-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:73:[L4] The root test: for a family from $1$, $\limsup_k |a_{k+1}|^{1/(k+1)} < 1$ gives convergence of $\sum_{k \ge 1}|a_k|$ and hence of $\sum_{k \ge 1} a_k$, and $> 1$ gives divergence of $\sum_{k \ge 1} a_k$ ([[thm-root-test]]).
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-74-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:75:[L5] The ratio test: $\limsup_k q_k < 1$ gives convergence of $\sum |a_k|$ and hence of $\sum a_k$, and $\liminf_k q_k > 1$ gives divergence of $\sum a_k$ ([[thm-ratio-test]]).
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-76-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-77-[L6] A series converges if and only if each of its tail series converges ([[lem-series-tail-invariance]], [[def-series]]).
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-78-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:79:[L7] For the sequence $a_k = 2^{-k} t_k$ built from the alternating sequence as in the Statement: $\limsup_k |a_{k+1}|^{1/(k+1)} = 1/2$, $\limsup_k |a_{k+1}|/|a_k| = 2$ and $\liminf_k |a_{k+1}|/|a_k| = 1/8$; and $2^{-k} > 0$, $t_k > 0$, so every term is positive and in particular nonzero ([[fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree]], [[lem-alternating-sequence]], [[def-integer-power]], [[lem-power-monotone]]).
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-80-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-81-## Proof
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-82-
--
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-84-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-85-1.1 Put $b_k := |a_k|$. Since $a_k \ne 0$ we have $b_k > 0$ for every $k$, so [L1] applies to $(b_k)$. [given, L3, L1]
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-86-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:87:1.2 For the sequence $a_k = 2^{-k} t_k$ of the Statement every term is nonzero, $\limsup_k \rho_k = 1/2 < 1$, and neither $\limsup_k q_k = 2 < 1$ nor $\liminf_k q_k = 1/8 > 1$ holds. [L7]
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-88-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:89:2.1 For this $(b_k)$ the ratio family is $b_{k+1}/b_k = |a_{k+1}|/|a_k| = q_k$ and the root family is $b_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)} = |a_{k+1}|^{1/(k+1)} = \rho_k$. [step 1.1]
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-90-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:91:3.1 Therefore $\liminf_k q_k \le \liminf_k \rho_k \le \limsup_k \rho_k \le \limsup_k q_k$, which is the displayed chain. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L1]
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-92-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:93:4.1 Suppose $\limsup_k q_k < 1$. By the chain, $\limsup_k \rho_k \le \limsup_k q_k < 1$, so the root test applies to the family $(a_k)_{k \ge 1}$ and gives convergence of $\sum_{k \ge 1}|a_k|$ and of $\sum_{k \ge 1} a_k$, hence of $\sum |a_k|$ and of $\sum a_k$; the ratio test gives the same conclusions. That is claim 1. [step 3.1, L4, L5, L6]
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-94-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:95:4.2 Suppose $\liminf_k q_k > 1$. By the chain and [L2], $\limsup_k \rho_k \ge \liminf_k \rho_k \ge \liminf_k q_k > 1$, so the root test gives divergence of $\sum_{k \ge 1} a_k$, hence of $\sum a_k$; the ratio test gives the same conclusion. That is claim 2. [step 3.1, L2, L4, L5, L6]
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-96-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:97:5.1 So for that sequence the root test gives convergence of $\sum_{k \ge 1} |a_k|$ while neither half of the ratio test applies, and the converse of claims 1 and 2 fails. [step 1.2, L4, L5] ∎
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-98-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-99-## Remarks
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-100-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:101:- **The dominance is a statement about $\limsup$, not about series.** The whole content is the chain of [[thm-ratio-root-inequality]], proved on the previous page precisely because it is about limits superior and nothing else. Claims 1 and 2 are the translation of that chain through the two tests, and they carry no further mathematics.
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-102-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:103:- **Strictly more, not merely at least as much.** The witness in the Statement settles that: its roots converge to $1/2$ while its ratios oscillate between $1/8$ and $2$, so the ratio test is silent about a series the root test decides. The reason is structural rather than accidental. Taking an $n$-th root divides the exponent by $n$ and so damps a bounded oscillation, while forming a ratio differences the exponent and preserves it.
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md-104-
items/cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test.md:105:- **The ratio test survives because it is easier to compute.** Nothing here says the ratio test should be abandoned; the ratios of a series given by an explicit formula are usually elementary, and the roots usually are not.
--
