items/rem-hereditary-and-productive-separation-ledger.md-29-
items/rem-hereditary-and-productive-separation-ledger.md:30:$T_0$, $T_1$, Hausdorffness, regularity, $T_3$, complete regularity, and Tychonoffness pass both to subspaces and to arbitrary products by [[thm-positive-heredity-of-separation-axioms]] and [[thm-positive-productivity-of-separation-axioms]]. The compound names retain their $T_1$ clauses: $T_3$ and Tychonoff are not alternative names for regularity and complete regularity.
items/rem-hereditary-and-productive-separation-ledger.md-31-
items/rem-hereditary-and-productive-separation-ledger.md-32-Normality has a narrower positive result: it passes to closed subspaces by [[lem-normality-is-closed-hereditary]]. Complete normality is exactly hereditary normality by [[thm-completely-normal-iff-hereditarily-normal]]. Under countable choice the deleted Tychonoff plank refutes hereditary normality ([[thm-normality-is-not-hereditary]]); under choice the lower-limit plane refutes productive normality ([[thm-normality-is-not-productive]]). These failures do not alter the positive ledger above.
--
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-41-
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-42-[L1] For $a>1$, $S_a(x):=\{a^q:q\in\mathbb Q,\ q<x\}$ and $a^{[x]}:=\sup S_a(x)$; for $0<a<1$, $a^{[x]}:=1/\bigl((a^{-1})^{[x]}\bigr)$; and $1^{[x]}:=1$ ([[def-real-power-by-rational-supremum]]).
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-43-
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md:44:[L2] Rational powers agree with exponential real powers, and $t\mapsto a^t$ is continuous; if $a>1$, then $\log a>0$, so $t\mapsto a^t=\exp(t\log a)$ is strictly increasing ([[thm-real-power-agrees-with-rational-exponent]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]], [[thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing]], [[def-real-power]]).
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-45-
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-46-[L3] Rational numbers are dense in $\mathbb R$, and the epsilon characterisation identifies a supremum of a nonempty bounded-above set ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]], [[lem-sup-epsilon]]).
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-47-
--
