items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-54-**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-55-
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md:56:1.1 Assume $a>1$. For every rational $q<x$, strict increase of $t\mapsto a^t$ gives $a^q<a^x$, so $a^x$ is an upper bound of $S_a(x)$. [L1, L2]
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-57-
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md:58:1.2 Given $\varepsilon>0$, continuity of $t\mapsto a^t$ at $x$ supplies $\delta>0$ such that $|t-x|<\delta$ implies $|a^t-a^x|<\varepsilon$; density supplies rational $q$ with $x-\delta<q<x$, hence $a^x-\varepsilon<a^q\in S_a(x)$. [L2, L3, choose]
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-59-
items/thm-rational-supremum-power-agrees-with-exp.md-60-2.1 By the supremum characterisation, steps 1.1 and 1.2 give $a^{[x]}=a^x$ when $a>1$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L3]
