items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-90-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-91-1.1 In $\mathbb{R}$ we have $2 = 1 + 1 > 0$, so [L1] supplies a unique $r \ge 0$ with $r^2 = 2$; and $r \ne 0$ because $r = 0$ would give $r^2 = 0 \ne 2$, so $r > 0$. Write $r =: \sqrt{2}$. [L1, L8, choose]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-92-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md:93:1.2 $S$ is nonempty: the rational $1$ satisfies $1 > 0$ and $1^2 = 1 < 2$, so $1 \in S$. [L8]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-94-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-95-1.3 Let $u \in \mathbb{Q}$ be an arbitrary upper bound of $S$ in $\mathbb{Q}$, that is, $q \le u$ for every $q \in S$. [assume-hyp]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-96-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-97-1.4 Let $\varepsilon > 0$ in $\mathbb{R}$ be arbitrary. [assume-hyp]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-98-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md:99:1.5 The rational $2$ is an upper bound of $S$ in $\mathbb{Q}$, so $S$ is bounded above there: for $q \in S$ order preservation gives $\iota(q) > 0$ and $\iota(q)^2 = \iota(q^2) < \iota(2) = 2 < 2 + 2 = 2 \cdot 2 = \iota(2)^2$, whence $\iota(q) < \iota(2)$ by [L2] and $q < 2$ by order reflection. [L2, L3, L8]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-100-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-101-2.1 $r$ is an upper bound of $\iota(S)$ in $\mathbb{R}$: for $q \in S$ order preservation gives $\iota(q) > 0$ and $\iota(q)^2 = \iota(q^2) < \iota(2) = 2 = r^2$, so $\iota(q) < r$ by [L2], hence $\iota(q) \le r$. [step 1.1, L2, L3, L8]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-102-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md:103:2.2 $r$ is not the image of any rational: if $r = \iota(q)$ then $\iota(q^2) = \iota(q)^2 = r^2 = 2 = \iota(2)$, so $q^2 = 2$ by injectivity of $\iota$, which [L5] forbids. [step 1.1, L3, L5]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-104-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-105-2.3 Put $a_\varepsilon := \max\{0,\ r - \varepsilon\}$, which exists by [L7]; then $0 \le a_\varepsilon$, and $a_\varepsilon < r$ because $a_\varepsilon$ is one of the two entries $0$ and $r - \varepsilon$, of which the first is $< r$ by 1.1 and the second is $< r$ because $\varepsilon > 0$. [step 1.1, step 1.4, L7, L8, choose]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-106-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-107-2.4 The image $\iota(u)$ of the bound $u$ is an upper bound of $\iota(S)$ in $\mathbb{R}$: for $q \in S$ the inequality $q \le u$ in $\mathbb{Q}$ gives $\iota(q) \le \iota(u)$ by order preservation. [step 1.3, L3]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-108-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md:109:3.1 Density applied to $a_\varepsilon < r$ produces a rational $q_\varepsilon$ with $a_\varepsilon < \iota(q_\varepsilon) < r$; then $\iota(q_\varepsilon) > a_\varepsilon \ge 0$ forces $q_\varepsilon > 0$ by order reflection, and $0 \le \iota(q_\varepsilon) < r$ gives $\iota(q_\varepsilon^2) = \iota(q_\varepsilon)^2 < r^2 = 2 = \iota(2)$ by [L2], hence $q_\varepsilon^2 < 2$ in $\mathbb{Q}$; so $q_\varepsilon \in S$ and $r - \varepsilon \le a_\varepsilon < \iota(q_\varepsilon)$. [step 2.3, step 1.1, L4, L2, L3]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-110-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-111-4.1 The set $\iota(S)$ is nonempty and bounded above in $\mathbb{R}$ by $r$, and for every $\varepsilon > 0$ its element $\iota(q_\varepsilon)$ satisfies $r - \varepsilon < \iota(q_\varepsilon)$; the epsilon characterisation therefore gives $\sup \iota(S) = r = \sqrt{2}$. [step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1, L6]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-112-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-113-5.1 Since $r$ is the least upper bound of $\iota(S)$ and $\iota(u)$ is an upper bound of it, we get $r \le \iota(u)$; and $r \ne \iota(u)$ by 2.2, so $r < \iota(u)$. [step 4.1, step 2.4, step 2.2, L8]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-114-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md:115:6.1 Density applied to $r < \iota(u)$ produces a rational $v$ with $r < \iota(v) < \iota(u)$; every $q \in S$ satisfies $\iota(q) \le r < \iota(v)$, so $q < v$ by order reflection and $v$ is an upper bound of $S$ in $\mathbb{Q}$; and $v < u$, again by order reflection. Hence $u$ is not $\le$ every upper bound of $S$ in $\mathbb{Q}$, so $u$ is not a supremum of $S$ in $\mathbb{Q}$. [step 5.1, step 2.1, L4, L3, L8]
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-116-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-117-7.1 The upper bound $u$ was an arbitrary one, so no upper bound of $S$ in $\mathbb{Q}$ is least: $S$ has a supremum in $\mathbb{R}$, equal to $\sqrt{2}$, and has no supremum in $\mathbb{Q}$ even though it is nonempty and bounded above there, for instance by $2$. [step 6.1, step 4.1, step 1.5, L8] ∎
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-118-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-119-## Remarks
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-120-
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md-121-- The set $S$ is bounded above in $\mathbb{Q}$, by $2$, as step 1.5 checks. Both hypotheses of the least-upper-bound property are therefore satisfied *inside* $\mathbb{Q}$, and the conclusion still fails. The property is a genuine assumption about the field, not a consequence of the order axioms alone.
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md:122:- The proof of leastness uses density twice, once to find an element of $S$ close below $\sqrt{2}$ and once to squeeze a rational between $\sqrt{2}$ and a putative rational least upper bound. Both uses go through [[lem-of-q-dense]], which is itself a consequence of the Archimedean property ([[thm-of-archimedean]]).
items/ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two.md:123:- The same argument runs with $2$ replaced by any positive rational $c$ that is not the square of a rational, once its two numerical steps are readjusted: step 1.2 must exhibit a positive rational whose square lies below $c$, and step 1.5 a positive rational whose square lies above it, neither of which is the constant $1$ or $2$ in general. Both exist for every positive rational $c$, and uniformly, so nothing here depends on $c$ being convenient: take $p := c/(1+c)$ and $w := 1 + c$, both positive rationals. Then $p^2 < c$, since $p^2 < c$ is equivalent to $c^2 < c(1+c)^2$ and hence, dividing by $c > 0$, to $c < (1+c)^2 = 1 + 2c + c^2$, which holds because $1 + c + c^2 > 0$; and $w^2 = 1 + 2c + c^2 > c$ for the same reason. Everything after those two steps is unchanged, so the failure is pervasive rather than a curiosity attached to $\sqrt{2}$.
--
