items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-63-[L2] Powers of $2$: $2^{-k} > 0$, $2^{-(k+1)} = 2^{-k}/2$, and $2^{-k} = 1/2^{k}$ ([[def-integer-power]], [[lem-power-laws]], [[lem-power-monotone]]).
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-64-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md:65:[L3] Limit superior in $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$: $\limsup_k x_k = \inf\{s_n\}$ with $s_n = \sup\{x_k : k \ge n\}$; every subset of $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$ has a least upper bound and a greatest lower bound there; and both quantities exist for every sequence ([[def-limsup-liminf]], [[lem-extended-reals-complete]], [[def-extended-reals]], [[lem-limsup-exists]]).
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-66-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-67-[L4] The geometric series $\sum_{k} r^{k}$ converges for $|r| < 1$; scaling by a nonzero constant preserves convergence; and direct comparison applies to nonnegative terms ([[thm-geometric-series]], [[lem-series-linearity]], [[thm-direct-comparison-test]], [[lem-of-abs-value]]).
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-68-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md:69:[L5] The ratio test: $\liminf_k |a_{k+1}/a_k| > 1$ gives divergence, and that is the only divergence criterion it supplies ([[thm-ratio-test]]).
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-70-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md:71:[L6] The refuted claim: for every sequence of nonzero reals with $\limsup_k |a_{k+1}/a_k| \ge 1$, the series $\sum a_k$ diverges.
