items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-80-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-81-2.2 For every $k$, $0 < a_k = c_k 2^{-k} \le 3 \cdot 2^{-k}$. [step 1.1, L2]
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-82-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md:83:3.1 The ratios are $q_k := |a_{k+1}/a_k| = a_{k+1}/a_k = \dfrac{c_{k+1}}{c_k}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}$, so $q_k = (1/3)(1/2) = 1/6$ when $s_k = 1$ and $q_k = 3 \cdot (1/2) = 3/2$ when $s_k = -1$, using $s_{k+1} = -s_k$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L1, L2, algebra]
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-84-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-85-3.2 The geometric series $\sum_k (1/2)^{k}$ converges, since $|1/2| < 1$; hence so does $\sum_k 3 \cdot 2^{-k}$, and by comparison so does $\sum_k a_k$. [step 2.2, L2, L4]
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-86-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-87-4.1 For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ there is an index $k \ge n$ with $s_k = -1$, namely $k = o_n$, since $o_n \ge n$; so $q_k = 3/2$ at some index $k \ge n$, for every $n$. [step 3.1, L1]
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-88-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md:89:5.1 Therefore every tail supremum satisfies $\sup\{q_k : k \ge n\} \ge 3/2$, so $3/2$ is a lower bound of the set of tail suprema and $\limsup_k q_k \ge 3/2 \ge 1$. [step 4.1, L3]
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-90-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md:91:6.1 So $(a_k)$ has nonzero terms and $\limsup_k |a_{k+1}/a_k| \ge 1$, yet $\sum a_k$ converges; the claim fails for it and is therefore false. [step 2.1, step 5.1, step 3.2, L6]
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-92-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md:93:7.1 Nothing in the ratio test is contradicted: its divergence half requires $\liminf_k q_k > 1$, and here $\liminf_k q_k \le 1/6 < 1$, since $q_k = 1/6$ at indices $e_n \ge n$ for every $n$. [step 6.1, step 3.1, L1, L3, L5] ∎
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-94-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-95-## Remarks
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-96-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md:97:- **Replacing $\liminf$ by $\limsup$ is already fatal, even with the inequality kept strict.** The witness above has $\limsup_k q_k \ge 3/2$, which is strictly greater than $1$, and its series converges. So the false claim is not rescued by demanding $\limsup_k |a_{k+1}/a_k| > 1$: the two quantities $\liminf$ and $\limsup$ are genuinely different hypotheses here, and only the first one works.
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md-98-
items/fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges.md:99:- **The asymmetry of the ratio test is not an accident of its proof.** A large ratio occurring arbitrarily late says only that the terms grow at those steps; it says nothing about their size, because they may have been made very small in between. Only an eventual lower bound on the ratios forces the terms to stay away from $0$, and that is precisely a hypothesis on $\liminf$.
--
items/ex-the-cofinite-topology-in-the-separation-hierarchy.md-91-
items/ex-the-cofinite-topology-in-the-separation-hierarchy.md-92-## Remarks
items/ex-the-cofinite-topology-in-the-separation-hierarchy.md-93-
items/ex-the-cofinite-topology-in-the-separation-hierarchy.md:94:- **The three failures have one cause.** In the cofinite topology on an infinite set the open sets are so large that no two nonempty ones are disjoint, so every separation axiom whose conclusion is a pair of disjoint nonempty open sets fails at once. What survives is $T_1$, whose conclusion asks for open sets that are allowed to overlap.
items/ex-the-cofinite-topology-in-the-separation-hierarchy.md-95-
items/ex-the-cofinite-topology-in-the-separation-hierarchy.md-96-- **The hypothesis that $X$ is infinite is necessary.** On a finite set the cofinite topology is the discrete one ([[def-standard-topologies]]), which satisfies every axiom on the main page.
items/ex-the-cofinite-topology-in-the-separation-hierarchy.md-97-
