items/thm-ratio-test.md-53-
items/thm-ratio-test.md-54-## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-ratio-test.md-55-
items/thm-ratio-test.md:56:**Given:** A sequence $(a_k)$ of reals with $a_k \ne 0$ for every $k$; the ratios $q_k = |a_{k+1}|/|a_k|$; the tail bounds $s_n = \sup\{q_k : k \ge n\}$ and $i_n = \inf\{q_k : k \ge n\}$ taken in $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$, so that $\limsup_k q_k = \inf\{s_n\}$ and $\liminf_k q_k = \sup\{i_n\}$ ([[def-limsup-liminf]], [[def-extended-reals]]); and the assumption that one of the two hypotheses of the Statement holds.
items/thm-ratio-test.md-57-
items/thm-ratio-test.md-58-[L1] Every subset of $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$ has a least upper bound and a greatest lower bound there, and the extended order is total ([[lem-extended-reals-complete]]). In particular $\inf\{s_n\} \le s_n$ and $\sup\{i_n\} \ge i_n$ for every $n$; $q_k \le s_n$ and $q_k \ge i_n$ for every $k \ge n$; a real $t$ exceeding $\inf\{s_n\}$ is not a lower bound of $\{s_n\}$; and a real $u$ below $\sup\{i_n\}$ is not an upper bound of $\{i_n\}$.
items/thm-ratio-test.md-59-
items/thm-ratio-test.md:60:[L2] Both quantities exist for every sequence ([[lem-limsup-exists]], [[def-limsup-liminf]]).
items/thm-ratio-test.md-61-
items/thm-ratio-test.md-62-[L3] Absolute value: $|x| \ge 0$; $|x| = 0$ exactly when $x = 0$; $|xy| = |x||y|$, so $|a_{k+1}/a_k| = |a_{k+1}|/|a_k|$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
items/thm-ratio-test.md-63-
--
items/thm-ratio-test.md-67-
items/thm-ratio-test.md-68-[L6] Direct comparison: if $0 \le x_j \le y_j$ from some index on and $\sum y_j$ converges then $\sum x_j$ converges ([[thm-direct-comparison-test]]).
items/thm-ratio-test.md-69-
items/thm-ratio-test.md:70:[L7] If a series converges then its terms tend to $0$ ([[lem-nth-term-test]], [[def-real-limit]]); and for every real $c > 0$ there is a natural $n \ge 1$ with the rational $1/n < c$ ([[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]]).
items/thm-ratio-test.md-71-
items/thm-ratio-test.md-72-[L8] Powers: $t^{0} = 1$, $t^{j+1} = t^{j} t$, and $t^{j} > 0$ for $t > 0$ ([[def-integer-power]], [[lem-power-monotone]]).
items/thm-ratio-test.md-73-
--
items/thm-ratio-test.md-77-
items/thm-ratio-test.md-78-**Proof technique:** cases.
items/thm-ratio-test.md-79-
items/thm-ratio-test.md:80:1.1 Assume $\limsup_{k} q_k < 1$. [assume-case lt]
items/thm-ratio-test.md-81-
items/thm-ratio-test.md-82-1.2 Assume instead $\liminf_{k} q_k > 1$. [assume-case gt]
items/thm-ratio-test.md-83-
items/thm-ratio-test.md:84:1.3 Each $q_k$ is a nonnegative real, being a quotient of a nonnegative real by a positive one, so every $s_n \ge q_n \ge 0$ and hence $\limsup_k q_k \ge 0$. [given, L1, L2, L3]
items/thm-ratio-test.md-85-
items/thm-ratio-test.md:86:2.1 In the case $\limsup_k q_k < 1$ the value $\Lambda := \limsup_k q_k$ therefore lies strictly between the reals $0$ and $1$ inclusive of $0$, so it is a real number; put $t := (\Lambda + 1)/2$, a real with $0 \le \Lambda < t < 1$. [step 1.1, step 1.3, L1, choose]
items/thm-ratio-test.md-87-
items/thm-ratio-test.md-88-2.2 In the case $\liminf_k q_k > 1$, the real $1$ is not an upper bound of $\{i_n\}$, so there is $N \in \mathbb{N}$ with $i_N > 1$. [step 1.2, L1, choose]
