items/thm-ratio-test.md-113-
items/thm-ratio-test.md-114-## Remarks
items/thm-ratio-test.md-115-
items/thm-ratio-test.md:116:- **The two halves are not dual, and the asymmetry is real.** Convergence needs the ratios to be *eventually* below a fixed $t < 1$, which $\limsup < 1$ supplies; divergence needs them *eventually* above $1$, which is what $\liminf > 1$ supplies. A hypothesis on $\limsup$ alone can never force divergence, since a single large ratio occurring arbitrarily late says nothing about the size of the terms. That is exactly what [[fs-ratio-limsup-at-least-one-diverges]] records.
items/thm-ratio-test.md-117-
items/thm-ratio-test.md:118:- **The geometric series is the only convergent series the proof knows.** Claim 1 is a comparison against $|a_N| t^{j}$, and every later refinement on this page, Kummer's test included, exists because that comparison is too coarse when the ratios approach $1$.
--
