items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md:92:2.1 The ratios are $q_k = a_{k+1}/a_k = \big(2^{-(k+1)} t_{k+1}\big)/\big(2^{-k} t_k\big) = 2^{-1} t_{k+1}/t_k$, which by step 1.2 equals $2^{-1}(1/2)/2 = 1/8$ when $s_k = 1$ and $2^{-1} \cdot 2/(1/2) = 2$ when $s_k = -1$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L4, L7, algebra]
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md-93-
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md-94-2.2 The roots are $r_k = \big(2^{-(k+1)} t_{k+1}\big)^{1/(k+1)} = \big(2^{-(k+1)}\big)^{1/(k+1)} t_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)} = 2^{-1} t_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)}$. [step 1.1, L4]
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md-95-
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md-96-2.3 Since $1/2 \le t_{k+1} \le 2$ for every $k$ and $x \mapsto x^{1/(k+1)}$ is nondecreasing on the positive reals, $(1/2)^{1/(k+1)} \le t_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)} \le 2^{1/(k+1)}$; both bounding sequences converge to $1$ by [L5], so the squeeze theorem gives $t_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)} \to 1$. [step 1.1, L4, L5, L6, L7]
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md-97-
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md:98:3.1 By steps 1.2 and 1.3 the tail range of $(q_k)$ at every index $n$ is exactly $\{1/8, 2\}$: those are the only values, and each occurs at some index $\ge n$. Its least upper bound in $\overline{\mathbb{R}}$ is $2$ and its greatest lower bound is $1/8$, since $1/8 < 2$ and both belong to the set; hence $\limsup_k q_k$ is the greatest lower bound of $\{2\}$, namely $2$, and $\liminf_k q_k$ is the least upper bound of $\{1/8\}$, namely $1/8$. [step 2.1, step 1.3, L2, L7]
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md-99-
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md:100:3.2 By steps 2.2 and 2.3 and the scalar rule, $r_k = 2^{-1} t_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)} \to 2^{-1} \cdot 1 = 1/2$, so $\limsup_k r_k = \liminf_k r_k = 1/2$. [step 2.2, step 2.3, L3, L6]
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md-101-
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md:102:4.1 For this sequence the claim asserts $\limsup_k r_k = \limsup_k q_k$, that is $1/2 = 2$; but $1/2 < 1 < 2$, so the two are different and the claim fails. [step 3.1, step 3.2, L7, L9]
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md-103-
items/fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree.md-104-5.1 The claim is therefore false. The true chain [L8] reads here $1/8 \le 1/2 \le 1/2 \le 2$, so both outer inequalities are strict for this witness while the middle one is an equality. [step 4.1, step 3.1, step 3.2, L7, L8, L9] ∎
