items/thm-ratio-root-inequality.md-138-
items/thm-ratio-root-inequality.md-139-- **The chain also explains the practical rule.** When
items/thm-ratio-root-inequality.md:140:  $\limsup_k q_k < 1$ the chain forces $\limsup_k r_k < 1$, so any conclusion
items/thm-ratio-root-inequality.md-141-  drawn from the roots is available from the ratios; but
items/thm-ratio-root-inequality.md:142:  $\limsup_k r_k < 1$ can hold with $\limsup_k q_k > 1$, and then only the root
items/thm-ratio-root-inequality.md-143-  side is usable. This is the sense in which the root criterion is the stronger
items/thm-ratio-root-inequality.md-144-  of the two.
